Wrong on both size and risk tier, and the error was too small. It was opened as a medium-size, medium-risk bug fix with one designer. It ran 6 spikes, 8 designs, 8 critiques, 11 builds, 13 code reviews and 6 validations. It bundled three separable pieces:
1. a medium scanner/App change (exclusion, refusal, Settings switch, report fields);
2. a large person-run measurement harness (PoolWatch -Prepare/-Protocol/-Rehearse/-Report);
3. an investigation of why pool reached 30 GB.
The slice's DONE (c) needed a person's Restart plus ten whole-C: scans, which the loop cannot run, so the slice could not close on its own evidence. The risk tier also missed that verification itself consumes kernel pool. The engine's 0.5 GB guard killed build laps #1 (+0.54 GB) and #10 (+0.79 GB). Splitting at open into fix, harness and measurement would have saved most of the rework.
